    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nIn `resolveRegistrationDate` (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:589`), I added a rejection for a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server's current date:\n\n```java\nprivate void resolveRegistrationDate(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {\n    LocalDate effective = ownerFieldsDto.getRegistrationDate();\n    if (effective == null) {\n        effective = LocalDate.now();\n    }\n    else if (effective.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n        throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"registrationDate\"));\n    }\n    ownerFieldsDto.setRegistrationDate(toBusinessDay(effective));\n}\n```\n\n## Why this approach\n\n- It follows the existing repo idiom: other field validations (`normalizeEmail`, `validatePostcode`) throw `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, which `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already maps to **400 Bad Request** with an `errors` array naming the offending field.\n- The check only applies to a *supplied* value (the `else if` branch). A missing `registrationDate` still defaults to today, so existing behavior is preserved.\n- The check runs on the raw supplied value *before* the business-day roll, so it's the user's actual input that's judged against the server date.\n- I updated the method's Javadoc to document the new rejection rule.\n\n## Test results\n\n`BUILD SUCCESS` \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (which posts `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"` and expects 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
